      en: "The Rub' al Khali, also known as the 'Empty Quarter', is the largest contiguous sand desert on Earth, covering approximately one-third of the Arabian Peninsula. The desert spans parts of Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Oman, and Yemen. Covering an area of about 650,000 square kilometres, it is larger than France. The landscape is dominated by massive sand dunes that can reach heights of up to 300 metres, such as the famous Moreeb Dune in the Liwa Oasis. The climate is extremely hyper-arid, with summer temperatures frequently exceeding 50 degrees Celsius. Despite the hostile conditions, the Rub' al Khali holds significant oil reserves beneath its vast sea of sand. Historically, the desert was a major obstacle for caravans, but today it is frequented by modern explorers and tourists. The silence and vastness of the red sandscapes make this place one of the most impressive natural wonders in the world.",
